body:
### Tested versions

Reproducible in Godot 4.6.1 stable on .NET
and reproducible on normal Godot 4.x since I'm pretty sure [this](https://godotforums.org/d/41745-meshinstance3d-randomly-disappearing-on-transform-changes) person ran into the exact same bug a few months ago.

### System information

Windows 11 - Godot 4.6.1 stable - Vulkan (Forward+) 

### Issue description

When using the [KidsCanCode - Align With Surface](https://kidscancode.org/godot_recipes/3.x/3d/3d_align_surface/index.html) code to edit the basis of a Node3D to align with the collision surface, it will set the scale to zero with no error or warning in the console. 

It looks like it only occurs if Node3D rotates on each perpendicular axis sequentially (i.e Vector3.Forward, Vector3.Right, Vector3.Up). Which way the collision is facing globally doesn't actually matter as long as they're perpendicular from each other

The KidsCanCode code used:
```
func align_with_y(xform, new_y):
    xform.basis.y = new_y
    xform.basis.x = -xform.basis.z.cross(new_y)
    xform.basis = xform.basis.orthonormalized()
    return xform
```

### Steps to reproduce

1. Open up Godot.
2. Create a 3D scene
4. Add multiple collision objects (Any type cause this bug to occur) in such a way that perpendicular geometry is easy to come across with the mouse cursor
5. Add a Camera3D pointing at said Collision objects
6. Add a Node3D that will act as the "Tester" node. Either give it a MeshInstance3D or make it a MeshInstance3D for easy viewing of this bug. I made it a cube.
7. Change the MeshInstance3D Size such that it'd be easy to see it changing orientation (I did (.3, .09, ,3))
8. Add a script to the Node3D:GDscript:
```
extends Node3D

var camera : Camera3D
var world_mouse_pos : Vector3

# I set my collision mask and all collisions to 2
@export_flags_3d_physics var collision_mask

func _ready() -> void:
	camera = get_window().get_camera_3d()

func _process(_delta: float) -> void:
	# Standard raycast
	var space_state = camera.get_world_3d().direct_space_state
	var mouse_pos : Vector2 = get_window().get_mouse_position()

	var origin : Vector3 = camera.project_ray_origin(mouse_pos)
	var end : Vector3 = origin + camera.project_ray_normal(mouse_pos) * 1000
	var query = PhysicsRayQueryParameters3D.create(origin, end)
	query.collision_mask = collision_mask

	var result : Dictionary = space_state.intersect_ray(query)
	var new_normal : Vector3
	if result:
		# if the raycast hit something
		world_mouse_pos = result["position"]
		new_normal = result["normal"]
	else:
		# if it didn't, default to an invisible plane in front of the camera
		var dragging_plane : Plane = Plane(camera.basis.z, .2)
		new_normal = camera.basis.z
		end = camera.project_ray_normal(mouse_pos)
		world_mouse_pos = dragging_plane.intersects_ray(origin, end)

	# set the Transform and Position
	global_transform = align_with_y(global_transform, new_normal)
	global_position = world_mouse_pos

func align_with_y(xform : Transform3D, new_y : Vector3) -> Transform3D:
	xform.basis.y = new_y
	xform.basis.x = -xform.basis.z.cross(new_y)
	xform.basis = xform.basis.orthonormalized()
	return xform
```
C#:
```
using Godot;
using Godot.Collections;
using System;

public partial class Tester2 : Node3D
{
    // I set my collision mask all collisions to 2
    [Export(PropertyHint.Layers3DPhysics)]
    private int CollisionMask { get; set; }

    private Camera3D camera;
    private Vector3 worldMousePos;

    public override void _Ready()
    {
        camera = GetWindow().GetCamera3D();
    }

    public override void _Process(double delta)
    {
        // standard raycast
        var spaceState = camera.GetWorld3D().DirectSpaceState;
        Vector2 mousePos = GetWindow().GetMousePosition();

        Vector3 origin = camera.ProjectRayOrigin(mousePos);
        Vector3 end = origin + camera.ProjectRayNormal(mousePos) * 100;

        PhysicsRayQueryParameters3D query = PhysicsRayQueryParameters3D.Create(origin, end);
        query.CollisionMask = (uint)CollisionMask;

        Dictionary result = spaceState.IntersectRay(query);
        Vector3 newNormal;
        if (result.Count != 0)
        {
            // if raycast hit something
            worldMousePos = (Vector3)result["position"];
            newNormal = (Vector3)result["normal"];
        }
        else 
        {
            // if it didn't, default to an invisible plane in forn of the camera
            Plane draggingPlane = new(camera.Basis.Z, .2f);
            newNormal = camera.Basis.Z;

            Vector3 to = camera.ProjectRayNormal(mousePos);
            worldMousePos = (Vector3)draggingPlane.IntersectsRay(origin, to);
        }

        // Set transform and position of Node3D
        GlobalTransform = AlignWithY(GlobalTransform, newNormal);
        GlobalPosition = worldMousePos;
    }


    private Transform3D AlignWithY(Transform3D trans, Vector3 newY)
    {
        Transform3D newTransform = trans;
        newTransform.Basis.Y = newY;
        newTransform.Basis.X = -newTransform.Basis.Z.Cross(newY);
        newTransform.Basis = newTransform.Basis.Orthonormalized();

        return newTransform;
    }

}
```
8. Run scene and move mouse cursor along collisions. Do so in such a way that object will be rotated perpendicularly in 3 different axis's (such as Vector3.Right, Vecotr3.Up, Vector3.Forward).
9. You should now see that the MeshInstance3D has disappeared. Going into the Remote tab will show that the Node3D's scale has been set to Vector3.zero.

For some reason I can't seem to be able to share a video I took of the bug, so I made a Reddit [post](https://www.reddit.com/user/Live-Common1015/comments/1s0rzmv/godot_bug_video/?utm_source=share&utm_medium=web3x&utm_name=web3xcss&utm_term=1&utm_content=share_button) with the video on it.

There is a very easy way of getting around this bug btw. Because it's an issue of the scale being changed, create a temporary Transform3D variable and take its rotational value to be applied to node3d's rotation:
GDscript:
```
var new_transform : Transform3D = align_with_y(global_transform, new_normal)
rotation = new_transform.basis.get_euler()
```
C#:
```
Transform3D newTransform = AlignWithY(GlobalTransform, newNormal);
Rotation = newTransform.Basis.GetEuler();
```

